WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:04.500
JAMES MILIUS: My
name is James Milius.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.330
I'm a senior here at
Hillsdale, originally

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.600
from Beaverton, Oregon.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.580
I'm studying philosophy
and sociology.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.050
And I'm here to introduce
our speaker for tonight.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.350
Dr. Frank Dikotter is
Chairman and Professor

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.570
of Humanities at the
University of Hong Kong

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.180
and Senior Fellow at
the Hoover Institution.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.710
Previously, he
taught at the School

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.710
of Oriental and African
Studies, University of London,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.720
from which he earned his PhD.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.540
He has written numerous books,
including The Discourse of Race

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.960
in Modern China, China Before
Mao: The Age of Openness,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.550
and Crime, Punishment and
the Prison in Modern China.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.500
Dr. Dikotter is the author of
People's Trilogy, a work that

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.270
documents the
impact of Communism

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.820
on the lives of ordinary
people in China.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.950
The first volume,
Mao's Great Famine,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.820
won the 2011 Samuel Jonsson
Prize for non-fiction.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.980
The second, The
Tragedy of Liberation,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.430
was shortlisted for the
Orwell Prize in 2014.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.800
The final installment,
The Cultural Revolution,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.135
was shortlisted for the PEN
Hessell-Tiltman Prize in 2017.

00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:04.510
Would you please join me in
welcoming Dr. Frank Dikotter?

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.300
FRANK DIKOTTER: Good evening.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.130
Thank you for coming.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.830
I'm delighted to be here,
a little jet lagged.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.260
I arrived from London yesterday.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.460
I got up at 5:00 in the morning.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.790
I couldn't wait any longer.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:51.810
So at 7:00, I went outside
and walked around the campus.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.560
So here's a fact.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.580
There's nobody to be seen in
Hillsdale on a Sunday morning

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.530
at 7:00.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.310
Not a soul.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.610
I did see a few chaps--

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:11.000
one of them is called George--

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.580
and a very distinguished
lady called Maggie.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.450
Now if 30 years
ago, somebody would

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.390
have asked Margaret
Thatcher, what

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.330
is the Organization Department
in the People's Republic

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.010
of China?

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:40.600
Or for that matter, the
Organization Bureau,

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.710
or [RUSSIAN],, as the
Russians used to say--

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.080
I think she would have frowned.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.970
I think she would
have said, young man,

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:56.150
back you go to Communism 101.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.140
About a month ago, at
the Hoover Institution,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:07.050
I was asked to speak to 20
senior Congressional Fellows,

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.680
all very smart, all
quite distinguished.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.180
I asked them, do you know what
the Organization Department

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.770
is in the People's
Republic of China?

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.570
Not a single one of
them knew what that was.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:31.500
So understanding China is
the theme of this conference.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.200
If you do not know what an
organization department is,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.850
you do not understand China.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.765
It's as simple as that.

00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:40.390
If you do know, you
don't have to stay.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:46.390
But there's good news.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.380
At the end of my talk,
you will know what it is,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:57.850
and I hope you will have a
better idea of the entity

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:01.550
that we are dealing with.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.820
My brief is to talk about
the Chinese Revolution, which

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.710
starts in 1949.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:11.690
I will focus mainly on
the 30 years under Mao,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.650
but I will also
spend about eight,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.440
nine minutes talking about
China over the last 40 years,

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.410
bringing it up to date.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:31.230
Now like the Organization
Department, or the [RUSSIAN],,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:35.610
everything invariably
starts in the Soviet Union.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.460
In this case, it
starts with Stalin.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:39.840
No Stalin, no Mao.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.530
It's as simple as that.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:50.210
On the 6th of August,
1945, Hiroshima in Japan

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:56.640
is erased in a blinding
flash of light.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.040
Two days later, Stalin sends a
million men across the border

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.170
from Siberia into Manchuria,
the industrial powerhouse

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.900
of China to the
north of Beijing.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.620
They are on their
way to Korea, where

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.680
they wait for the American
allies along the 38th parallel.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.720
Japan surrenders.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.030
The Russians don't leave.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.870
They hand over North
Korea to Kim Il-Sung

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.840
and the countryside
in Manchuria to

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.620
the Chinese Communist
leader, Mao Tse Tung.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.690
They help him transform
his ragtag army

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:47.320
of guerrilla fighters into a
formidable fighting machine.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:52.250
The Russians establish
16 military institutions,

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:58.120
including Air Force, artillery,
and engineering schools.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.700
Some Chinese officers
go all the way

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.610
to Moscow for advanced training.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:12.560
Logistical aid arrives
by rail and by sea.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.790
In North Korea alone,
some 2,000 wagon loads

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:19.550
are allocated to the task.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.640
The Americans, on
the other hand,

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.390
impose an arms embargo on the
wartime ally, Chiang Kai-shek,

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:33.910
and the nationalists.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:39.780
Chiang Kai-shek loses
battle after battle

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:43.060
in a war of attrition
in Manchuria.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.420
By 1948, the Communists
feel strong enough

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:53.960
to start surrounding
cities and blockading them.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:00.690
In the case of Changchun, Lin
Biao, one of Mao's generals,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:05.520
commands the city
to be transformed

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.980
into a city of death.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:14.470
His troops establish a perimeter
around Changchun, trenches

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.410
four meters deep, and
do not allow anyone

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.000
to leave for five months.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:26.500
160,000 ordinary people
are starved to death.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:33.800
[INAUDIBLE],, a lieutenant with
the Chinese Communist Army,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:41.955
noted later on that Hiroshima
and Changchun were the same.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.840
Hiroshima took nine seconds.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.670
Changchun took five months.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.550
The casualties were
roughly the same.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.720
Other cities surrender,
unwilling to undergo

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.430
the same fate.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.670
By the end of 1949, Chiang
Kai-shek and the Nationalists

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.860
flee to Taiwan.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:14.470
The red flag goes up over the
Forbidden City in Beijing.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.510
It is called
liberation, a term that

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:25.200
brings to mind jubilant crowds
who welcomed the liberators.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:36.950
But liberation is the result
of a long and brutal war

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:37.490
of conquest.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:49.850
Power, Mao used to say, power
comes from the barrel of a gun.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:56.270
But Mao knew very well that the
People's Liberation Army could

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.110
only keep power for so long.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:06.020
What was required
was a revolution,

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:11.010
the overthrow of the old order.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:20.100
And it came in the guise of
a campaign for land reform.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.230
It had very little
to do with the land.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:27.180
It had everything
to do with power.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.860
Communist work teams
fanned out into villages

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.400
and polarized closely
knit communities

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:43.910
into a majority obliged to
denounce a minority of victims,

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:49.570
frequently the democratically
elected village leaders.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:53.030
Some villages did
this with relish,

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:57.620
but a great many had no choice
because those who refused

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.050
would be denounced in turn.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.900
The victims were
denounced as landlords,

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:12.650
tyrants, evil gentry,
humiliated, beaten,

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:18.950
sometimes killed, their assets
distributed to the crowd.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:26.500
So in effect, ordinary
people entered a pact

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.710
sealed in blood with the party.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.250
All of them had
blood on their hands.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:38.380
All of them had participated
in the persecution

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:42.290
of a small number of victims.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:44.990
They did not want
Chiang Kai-shek

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:48.710
and the Nationalist
party to return.

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:50.930
This is land reform.

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:54.890
A year later, in October,
1950, Chairman Mao

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.770
decided that it was
time to eliminate

00:10:57.770 --> 00:11:01.520
all the enemies standing in
the path of the revolution--

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:05.550
spies, bandits, counter
revolutionaries.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:12.750
He issued a killing quota
of one per thousand.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.210
Go out, kill one per thousand.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:21.570
In many parts of
China, local cadres--

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:24.470
some pronounce this as cadres--

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.740
local cadres, or
Communist Party members,

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:31.640
tried to vie with
each other and show

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:35.000
their revolutionary
determination

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.880
so that this quota
doubled or trebled.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:44.810
In the south, some of
these party activists

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:48.200
used the campaign of
terror to settle accounts

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.810
with the local population
who had resisted

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:53.420
the arrival of the Communists.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:56.030
Entire villages were razed.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:59.570
In some cases, schoolchildren
as young as six

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:04.990
were accused of spying
for the Nationalists.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.390
Sometimes a few
prisoners were simply

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:11.470
taken out and shot
for a local official

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:17.170
to meet his killing quota
of one per thousand.

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:23.730
People were supposed to be
shot in public and rallies

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:28.370
taking place in stadiums,
which all had to witness.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.530
But in many cases,
they were taken

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:36.480
deep into forests, along
rivers, near ravines,

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:37.800
alone or in batches.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:47.120
About 1.5 to two million
people were eliminated,

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:53.750
coming on top of the two million
people denounced as landlords

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:56.300
during so-called land reform.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.450
So by now we're counting in the
four to five million victims.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.470
Many more were
stigmatized for life

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:09.380
and disappeared into a
sprawling prison system

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:12.410
across the country.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:18.600
To paraphrase Simon
Schama, the historian

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:24.950
of the French Revolution,
violence was the revolution.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:30.830
But of course, violence had
trusted companions, namely

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.820
fear and intimidation.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:38.130
People across the
realm were asked

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.980
to transform
themselves into what

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.770
the party called new people.

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:48.750
They had to go back to school
in factories and offices

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:56.300
and learn the right answers,
learn the right ideas,

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:01.750
learn how to shout the
right slogans, to confess,

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.180
account for their
past activities,

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.990
demonstrate their
loyalty to the party,

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:14.130
possibly denounce colleagues,
neighbors, family members.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:18.060
But of course, there's more than
just violence and intimidation.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:21.500
The history of
revolution in China

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:25.700
is also a history of promises
made and promises broken.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:35.160
Mao, like Lenin
and the Bolsheviks,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:41.040
promised every disaffected
group what they wanted most--

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.280
more land for the
villages, freedom

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:48.840
of speech for intellectuals,
independence for minorities,

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:54.450
protection of private
property for entrepreneurs,

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:57.510
better working conditions
for the workers.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:02.730
One by one, these
promises were destroyed

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:09.330
as Mao imposed a harsh
Stalinist system on the country.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:14.280
One by one, the enemies
were eliminated.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:19.010
It started first with the
counter revolutionaries

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:20.880
at the height of this
campaign of terror

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.670
that I just described.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:25.850
But then the campaign moved
on to target former government

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:29.690
servants who had been
promised lenient treatment

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:34.370
by the Communists in 1949 but
were eliminated or targeted

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:36.140
in 1952.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:41.960
And after that, the business
community was brought to heel.

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:46.370
Every organization outside
of the organization

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:49.580
of the Communist Party
of China was eliminated--

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:53.540
religious communities,
philanthropic associations,

00:15:53.540 --> 00:16:00.400
powerful chambers of
commerce, labor unions,

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:05.590
all of them eliminated
and replaced by the party.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:11.223
By 1956, all enterprises
were nationalized

00:16:11.223 --> 00:16:12.640
and became functions
of the state.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:17.750
If you had been running a
corner shop in Shanghai,

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.970
then you would be
pleading with the party

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:24.125
to be allowed to continue
to work as a state employee.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:30.090
The land was taken back
from all the villagers, who

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:31.860
lost their freedom of movement.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.970
By 1956, they had
to sell the grain

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:41.510
they produced to the state
at state-mandated prices.

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:46.120
But something happened in
1956 that upset the chairman.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:52.130
His model, Stalin, had
died three years earlier.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:57.710
In February, 1956, his
successor, Nikita Khrushchev,

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:01.640
in a secret speech that very
much shook the Socialist camp

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:06.410
to its core, denounced
Stalin, his reign and terror

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.230
and a cult of personality.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.710
Mao viewed it as
a personal attack.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:15.670
He, after all, was
China's Stalin.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:19.000
His answer was to come
up with a great leap

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:24.869
forward from socialism into
communism that would somehow

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:28.920
leave the Soviet Union behind.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:33.960
His hope was to upstage
Nikita Khrushchev

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.560
and become the leader
of the Socialist camp.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:45.800
Hundreds of millions of ordinary
people in the countryside

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:50.210
were herded into
giant collectives

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:53.640
called people's communes.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.260
These people's
communes were modeled

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:58.590
on the army with
collective canteens,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.390
collective dormitories.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:05.660
By transforming every man and
every woman in the countryside

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:09.170
into a foot soldier,
and a giant army

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:13.820
to be deployed day and night
to transform the economy,

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:18.620
Mao thought that he could
catapult his country

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.640
past the Soviet Union.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:23.765
But the Great Leap
Forward was a disaster.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:32.380
It led to literally
tens of millions

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:40.990
of people being starved,
beaten, worked to death.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:45.220
Villagers had no
incentive to work.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:48.840
The land was no longer theirs.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:53.260
Their tools, utensils,
even pots and pans

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:55.330
had all been confiscated.

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:59.230
They were literally bonded
servants of the state,

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:02.890
at the back and call
of local cadres.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:09.570
They earned work points to be
exchanged for food in canteens.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.630
But the cadres,
on the other hand,

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:16.760
had to whip up the workforce
in one relentless drive

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:17.510
after the other.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:22.910
Produce more fertilizer,
more grain, more steel

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:24.930
from backyard furnaces.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.970
They had to whip
up the workforce.

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:32.950
So the scope and
degree of coercion

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:38.560
had to be constantly expanded,
resulting in a mounting

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:42.780
spiral of violence.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:49.280
In order to get impoverished,
famished people to work,

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:54.170
they were pummeled, beaten,
whipped, some of them tied up,

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:58.260
thrown into ponds,
covered in excrement,

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:03.175
branded with sizzling tools,
occasionally buried alive.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:16.430
The most important tool at
the disposal of party cadres

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:22.510
to punish people who didn't
work hard enough was food.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:26.250
Food was used as a weapon.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.140
In many parts of
the country, those

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:32.940
who were too weak
or too old to work

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:37.590
were deliberately
banned from the canteens

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:41.300
and allowed to starve to death.

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:44.790
What I'm trying to say is
that people didn't simply

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:45.820
starve to death.

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:49.560
They were starved to death.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:55.910
The Great Leap Forward came to
an end in 1962 at great cost.

00:20:55.910 --> 00:21:02.410
And it damaged the
standing of Mao Tse Tung

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:11.760
He wondered whether somebody
might destroy his legacy,

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:14.550
as had happened with Stalin.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:20.280
Would there be a Chinese
Kruschev to denounce him,

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:24.630
even as he was still alive?

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:27.470
And most of all, how
would Chairman Mao

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:32.180
be able to spot the
Chinese Kruschev,

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:36.440
since Stalin himself had
been unable to identify

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.310
his own nemesis and had
even promoted Kruschev

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:43.170
through the ranks of the party?

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:46.980
Mao's response was the
Cultural Revolution.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:49.300
He prepared it very carefully.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.900
It began in 1966,
four years later.

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:58.640
The Cultural
Revolution was designed

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:07.710
to be an attack on feudal,
bourgeois, capitalist culture

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:14.180
that Mao believed was a
threat to Communist ideology.

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:17.000
But Mao also used the
Cultural Revolution

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:23.600
to unleash ordinary people
and punish all those

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:27.590
inside the party
hierarchy who had ever

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:33.580
expressed any doubt about him
and the Great Leap Forward.

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:37.420
First, students in
the summer of 1966

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:41.680
were allowed to
criticize their teachers.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:46.600
They rapidly became Red
Guards and also attacked

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:51.320
what they refer to
as the old rules,

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:58.870
burning books, vandalizing
churches, tearing down temples,

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:02.300
attacking any sign of the past.

00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:09.900
In Shanghai alone they raided
a quarter of a million homes,

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:14.290
taking away ordinary
books, family photographs,

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:21.030
but also antique bronzes, rare
paintings, all of it destroyed.

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:23.940
They also attacked
people they suspected

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:26.700
were enemies of the Revolution.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:31.500
In Beijing alone, in
September, 1966, 1,700

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:37.520
of them were killed, a few
thousand petrol and set alight,

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:41.340
a number also electrocuted.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.000
In the autumn of
1966, Mao went further

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.950
and allowed ordinary people
to criticize ranking party

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:54.090
members, which they did.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.060
But people, of
course, became divided

00:23:56.060 --> 00:24:00.830
over who the true enemies of
the Cultural Revolution were,

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:03.760
so they started
fighting each other.

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:07.930
Then the army intervened,
as Mao asked them to support

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:11.300
the true proletarian left.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:13.270
But different
officers in the Army

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:17.410
weren't quite sure, either, who
they should fight, and ended up

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:20.790
fighting each other
in the streets.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:28.480
The country was on the
very edge of civil war.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:31.650
This episode comes
to an end in 1968,

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:36.060
as so-called new Revolutionary
Party committees appear.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:38.400
They represent a
command structure

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:40.770
that Mao relished,
top to bottom,

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.820
one man in every place,
the military structure.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:48.000
And these so-called
Revolutionary Party committees

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:50.790
were heavily
dominated by the Army,

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:55.230
with soldiers overseeing
factories, schools, offices.

00:24:55.230 --> 00:25:00.300
They turned China
into a Garrison state.

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:04.800
Their first act was to punish
all the students and Red Guards

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:07.950
who had taken the
chairman at his word,

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:09.900
sending literally
millions of them

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:13.620
to the countryside to be
re-educated by the peasants.

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.810
Other purges followed.

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:19.890
In Shanghai, anyone with
a foreign link in the past

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:21.180
was seen to be suspect.

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:25.560
170,000 people were
harassed, thousands

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:28.920
killed or committing suicide.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:33.160
In Guangdong Province
alone, the province

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:36.850
just across the border from Hong
Kong, where I work and live,

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:43.340
one estimate puts the
body count at 40,000.

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:50.080
But of course, Mao was
suspicious of everyone,

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.285
and also the army that
now run the country.

00:25:56.030 --> 00:25:59.900
In September, 1971, Lin
Biao, the very man who

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:06.410
had besieged Changchun
in Manchuria in 1948,

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:10.100
conveniently died
in a plane accident.

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:15.120
Now the army was purged in turn.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:19.680
At this point, in 1971, ordinary
people in the countryside

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:25.140
realize that the Cultural
Revolution has badly damaged

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.120
the Communist Party of China.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:30.900
The credibility of communism
was destroyed by the Great Leap

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:34.650
Forward and the famine, but
the organization of the party

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:38.860
was undermined by the
Cultural Revolution.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:42.320
Ordinary people,
villagers, start

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:45.260
what I refer to as
a silent revolution.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:50.180
Even before Mao dies,
they take back the land.

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:52.400
They take back their tools.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.830
They open black markets.

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:58.290
They operate
underground factories.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:02.120
They return to the past.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:06.540
And there's not a lot the
party can do about it.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.890
No party would have
tolerated open confrontation.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.320
But these are
millions and millions

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:18.670
of daily acts of
resistance as people

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:23.050
wrenched away from the state
very basic economic freedoms.

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:28.500
Mao dies in 1976.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:32.880
Three years later, a man
referred to as Deng Xiaoping

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:34.350
take his place.

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:35.850
Short, nasty little man.

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:43.800
He had purged
intellectuals in 1957,

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:49.010
and he would do the same
again and again and again.

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:54.190
But Deng Xiaoping
at first attempted

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:59.340
to force villagers to go back
to the people's communes.

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:04.680
He soon realized that he
didn't have the clout to do it.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:08.940
So he's wise enough to step
back and allow ordinary people

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:12.555
to just get on with
their economic business.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:19.130
The people's communes
collapsed in 1982.

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:25.900
From here onwards, very
basic economic freedoms

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.350
produce economic growth.

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:35.140
That economic growth is used
by the party to rebuild itself.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:37.450
Deng Xiaoping and
his followers are

00:28:37.450 --> 00:28:44.040
determined to allow
some economic leniency,

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:48.120
but equally
determined to suppress

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:51.340
every political aspiration.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.870
There are many twists
and turns in this story,

00:28:54.870 --> 00:29:02.380
not least the massacre around
Tiananmen Square in June 1989.

00:29:02.380 --> 00:29:06.070
But overall, human
ingenuity managed

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:10.480
to thrive despite the
constraints of the one party

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:11.880
state.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:17.520
If you have acclaimed what has
happened since 1979 as nothing

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:22.760
short of an economic
miracle, well,

00:29:22.760 --> 00:29:30.600
if you look at the gross
domestic product per capita

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.850
according to the
International Monetary Fund,

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:38.990
China ranked number 126 in 1980.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:42.060
By the time the tanks move
into Tiananmen Square,

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.620
it has moved up to
121, where it remains

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:48.590
until the end of
the 20th century,

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:55.250
only to increase by
five ranks up to 2007.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:30:00.280
At that point, it is just
below Zimbabwe under Mugabe.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:02.220
He's gone.

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.760
Thank God for that.

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:10.210
And just below Zimbabwe,
just above Turkmenistan.

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:13.530
That's where China is in 2007.

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:18.310
It is true that after
the crisis in 2008,

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:23.020
it jumps all the
way up from 116,

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:24.910
jumps all the way up to 76.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:27.260
Quite an achievement.

00:30:27.260 --> 00:30:31.590
In fact, for anyone who
has visited China 10,

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:38.210
15, 20 years ago, it seems as
if it is a different country.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:41.390
And indeed, one of the
accomplishments of the regime

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:45.740
today is to have
distanced itself

00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:50.150
so successfully from the
Mao era that it doesn't even

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:52.110
appear to exist.

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:55.550
There are no monuments to
the victims of the Cultural

00:30:55.550 --> 00:31:01.010
Revolution, no memorials
for the tens of millions who

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:03.650
died during Mao's Great Famine.

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:07.030
But on the other hand,
skyscrapers everywhere,

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:13.890
bullet trains, modern
airports, modern railways,

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:17.740
roses along the highways.

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:20.840
It's almost like a
paradise, isn't it?

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:24.860
But when you scratch that
shimmering surface, what

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:29.900
you will discover underneath
are the iron underpinnings

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:32.240
of the one party state.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:38.260
The very institutions put
in place by Mao after 1949

00:31:38.260 --> 00:31:40.540
have been spruced up.

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:43.460
There is, for instance,
the Propaganda

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:48.560
Department, which hasn't
changed its function since Mao,

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:54.080
although today, conveniently,
it is translated officially

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:59.560
into English as the
Publicity Department.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:03.720
Then there is the
Ministry of Security

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:07.830
and the Ministry of State
Security, two giants that

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:12.000
reach out into every
single household.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.760
There's the Bureau
of Statistics that

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:20.700
produced the numbers required
to portray the Great Leap

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:23.510
Forward as a miracle.

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:25.260
And the Bureau of
Statistics continues

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:31.530
to produce the figures
that the party demands.

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:36.580
And then, of course, there is
the Organization Department

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:39.610
that anyone familiar
with communist regimes

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:43.280
will immediately
identify as the one that

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:49.040
is responsible for appointing
leading party members to shadow

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:52.690
every government institution.

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:57.820
If Hillsdale were to be
taken over by the Communist

00:32:57.820 --> 00:33:01.592
Party of China
tomorrow, God forbid,

00:33:01.592 --> 00:33:03.300
it would mean that
the mayor of Hillsdale

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:06.510
is appointed by the
Organization Department.

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:07.980
The president of
Hillsdale College

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:11.810
is appointed by the
Organization Department.

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:14.690
The Organization Department
will appoint a party secretary

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:18.161
to every department and
decide who teaches what,

00:33:18.161 --> 00:33:21.050
what should be researched,
and who should be invited.

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:22.760
My feeling is I
would not be welcome.

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:36.300
After 1949, Mao destroyed
all organizations outside

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:41.480
of the organization of the
Communist Party of China.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:45.730
He placed the
party in a position

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:50.260
to rob the people
of their assets.

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:54.790
That situation has not
fundamentally changed.

00:33:54.790 --> 00:33:58.510
The land belongs to the state.

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:01.870
The banks belong to the state.

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:04.720
Industry belongs to the state.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:07.960
Major enterprises are
controlled, directly

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:10.480
or indirectly, by the state.

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:16.610
Economists have observed
that consumption levels

00:34:16.610 --> 00:34:20.159
tend to be low in the
People's Republic of China.

00:34:20.159 --> 00:34:25.010
They are low because their
wealth goes to the state,

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:29.000
and from the state to the
party, not to ordinary people.

00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:38.270
Well, you might object and
say, it's not communist.

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:40.730
They allow private property.

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:45.610
Really.

00:34:45.610 --> 00:34:49.420
But every single leader
from Mao Tse Tung onwards,

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:52.960
including Deng Xiaoping,
has made it crystal clear

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:55.750
that there will not be
separation of powers,

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:57.380
and there is none.

00:34:57.380 --> 00:35:01.200
There is no judicial
independence,

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:04.180
which means that there
is no rule of law,

00:35:04.180 --> 00:35:07.400
which means that you cannot
defend your private property.

00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:09.930
If you read the
Constitution, you

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.580
will find out that
what is referred

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:16.390
to as Socialist property
is apparently sacred,

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:18.832
but not so private property.

00:35:18.832 --> 00:35:20.290
You might say, but
there are plenty

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:21.850
of billionaires in China.

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:27.490
Yes, they occasionally
disappear for a while.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.360
They either reappear
on TV to confess

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:32.200
or you never see them again.

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:35.260
Their assets now
belong to the state,

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:38.530
and other billionaires
then scurry

00:35:38.530 --> 00:35:44.100
to prove their
loyalty to the party.

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:49.410
In essence, despite all the
talk about reform and opening

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:54.600
up over the last 40
years, the Communist Party

00:35:54.600 --> 00:36:01.360
has remained in
charge of the economy.

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:03.840
The commanding
heights of the economy

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:07.150
are controlled by the party.

00:36:07.150 --> 00:36:11.980
You may very well
argue that there

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:17.550
are relative merits
to a one party state,

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:20.850
for instance, stability,
or at least the appearance

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:25.170
of stability during
economic crises,

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:30.010
for instance, the one in 2008.

00:36:30.010 --> 00:36:38.750
But to think that the Chinese
Communist Party will undertake

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:45.860
meaningful economic or political
reform, I think, is mistaken.

00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:54.630
Its hand is everywhere and will
not move of its own accord.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:59.610
To think otherwise, to believe
that political reform will

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:05.850
follow economic reform, as every
American president has done

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:10.720
from Nixon onwards, except for
the one in Washington today,

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:15.850
is to engage in what I
call a China fantasy.

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:17.182
Thank you.

00:37:17.182 --> 00:37:18.655
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:24.285 --> 00:37:25.160
SPEAKER 1: Thank you.

00:37:29.060 --> 00:37:31.610
Dr. Dikotter has
agreed to sign copies

00:37:31.610 --> 00:37:35.060
of his book, Mao's Great
Famine, directly following

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:38.540
this session in the
Surrell Lobby outside.

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:40.970
And we now have time
for a few questions,

00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:42.770
so please make your
way to the microphone

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:44.340
if you have a question.

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.510
And I am reminded
by our director

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:49.760
that students who
ask good questions

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:51.670
will be considered
for extra credit.

00:37:54.500 --> 00:37:57.220
OK.

00:37:57.220 --> 00:37:58.390
AUDIENCE: You say that--

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:00.070
I learned that you
are a professor

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:01.990
at the University of Hong Kong.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:04.780
How is teaching there
with the situation that's

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:06.910
going on right there right now?

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:09.090
FRANK DIKOTTER: It's
a good question,

00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:11.900
but I haven't taught there
the last three months.

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:13.990
In fact, I haven't
even been in Hong Kong.

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:16.640
I was at the Hoover
Institution in July and August,

00:38:16.640 --> 00:38:18.430
and I've been in Europe since.

00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:20.710
I was only in Hong
Kong for one day.

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:22.840
I was happy the
airport was open.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:26.620
Having said that, I have
nothing but admiration

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:28.620
for the people of Hong Kong.

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:30.820
And I have to say I have
slightly less admiration

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:32.200
for the police.

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:33.940
I understand that
occasionally, you

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:40.030
might wish to use tear gas and
fire rubber bullets and club

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:42.010
people on the head.

00:38:42.010 --> 00:38:43.960
But you must have a strategy.

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:46.300
The only result is that
these demonstrators move

00:38:46.300 --> 00:38:47.920
from one street to another.

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:49.660
It seems rather pointless.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.970
It fuels anger.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:55.300
It creates more protesters,
more grievances.

00:38:55.300 --> 00:38:57.280
What is crystal
clear is that many

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:59.830
of these very young people,
some of them probably

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:04.360
my own students, are
utterly determined

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:08.800
because they've seen whatever
few freedoms they have

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:13.030
being very gradually undermined
by the one party state

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:14.290
across the border.

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:17.800
In 1997, Hong Kong was
returned from Britain,

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:21.400
was a Crown colony, to the
People's Republic of China.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:23.680
People's Republic
of China undertook

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:27.040
to not change its
institutions for 50 years

00:39:27.040 --> 00:39:28.780
and promised free elections.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:30.400
That promise has been broken.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.500
Now those few freedoms
are being crushed.

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:35.630
People are upset,
and rightly so.

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:38.235
I have nothing but
admiration for them.

00:39:38.235 --> 00:39:39.110
SPEAKER 1: All right.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:40.740
Next question.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:42.390
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:45.280
From my limited knowledge
of Chinese history,

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:48.360
it seems that the regime has had
various weaknesses that you've

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:49.870
pointed to.

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:52.530
But with the recent
implementation

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:55.860
of their social credit system
and the massive surveillance

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:58.110
state that they've
built, it seems

00:39:58.110 --> 00:39:59.583
that they would
be able to correct

00:39:59.583 --> 00:40:01.500
a lot of these weaknesses,
and I wonder if you

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:04.200
might be able to speak to that.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:07.410
FRANK DIKOTTER: It's important
not to overestimate your enemy.

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:13.040
It's important not to
underestimate him, either.

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:15.160
We tend to think
of dictatorships

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:17.690
as societies which
are beautifully

00:40:17.690 --> 00:40:20.460
regimented from top to bottom.

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:28.010
The truth is, under the Nazis,
under Stalin, under Mao,

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:30.880
it is chaos.

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:32.620
Why?

00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:34.780
Because there is never
just one dictator.

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:36.730
There are a million of them.

00:40:36.730 --> 00:40:38.770
I'll be my little dictator here.

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:42.510
There are thousands of rules
and thousands of regulations.

00:40:42.510 --> 00:40:45.940
They're implemented according
to the whim of the person who

00:40:45.940 --> 00:40:47.710
happens to be in charge.

00:40:47.710 --> 00:40:50.190
It creates chaos on the ground.

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:52.560
It's true to this day.

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:53.830
I go to China--

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:58.390
this year, before July, I went
virtually every second week.

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:02.290
There are cameras
everywhere in Shanghai.

00:41:02.290 --> 00:41:10.030
People, we are told, will
be fined if they jaywalk.

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:11.900
I do it.

00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:17.590
Everybody does it
happily, except that there

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:21.370
is a policeman standing
right there to stop you.

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:24.520
But as he stops you,
somebody else will cross.

00:41:24.520 --> 00:41:26.500
Anarchy.

00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:28.900
When I travel, I'm
always a little

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:34.080
apprehensive
because these people

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.780
at airports, railway stations--

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:40.080
customs is across the border
from Hong Kong into China--

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:43.590
are obviously unable
to read a passport.

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:46.270
They don't know who I am.

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:48.280
How do you spell my name?

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:51.240
It's all a great mystery.

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:56.080
So before we get to
this Orwellian state

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:59.680
where everybody's
registered and regimented,

00:41:59.680 --> 00:42:02.350
I think China will
have a very long way

00:42:02.350 --> 00:42:06.630
to go with one exception.

00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:10.950
And that is, of course, the
outer realms of the empire.

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:14.850
The Bolsheviks inherited
the Czarist empire.

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:18.333
Mao inherited the Ching empire.

00:42:18.333 --> 00:42:19.500
The boundaries are the same.

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:22.280
They haven't decolonized yet.

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:25.070
So when it comes to
Tibet and Xinjiang,

00:42:25.070 --> 00:42:28.280
there are definitely
cameras everywhere.

00:42:28.280 --> 00:42:33.730
And every means possible
is used to punish people.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:35.620
There are more
cameras, probably,

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:37.210
in some villages
in Tibet than there

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:41.300
are textbooks for children.

00:42:41.300 --> 00:42:44.690
These are tremendously
poor villages,

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:47.290
yet you will see
cameras on every street.

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:49.290
So in that sense, yes,
it's definitely a threat.

00:42:52.403 --> 00:42:53.070
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:57.320
I have a quick question
regarding the Sin Kang

00:42:57.320 --> 00:43:01.010
Kang in the inner
Mongolia and Tibet.

00:43:01.010 --> 00:43:03.900
Sun Yat Sen, I believe
he said that one

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:07.020
of the reasons you couldn't
implement democracy into China

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:12.000
immediately was because
people weren't ready for--

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:14.220
sort of the country
wasn't unified yet.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:18.090
So somebody had to unify the
government first, and then

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:20.370
allow for freedom and elections.

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:23.820
Is this what the People's
Republic of China

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:28.830
is doing with their sinicization
of the SIn Kang, Inner

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:33.600
Mongolia, and Tibet in order
to prevent separatist movements

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:35.552
once they democratize?

00:43:35.552 --> 00:43:37.260
FRANK DIKOTTER: So
there's two questions.

00:43:37.260 --> 00:43:38.280
One is Sun Yat Sen.

00:43:38.280 --> 00:43:41.190
One is what happened after 1949.

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:44.130
Very clearly, this
is an empire that

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:47.290
pretends to be a nation state.

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:50.350
So what you see is
the same time zone,

00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:54.570
whether you traveled to
Xinjiang or to Manchuria,

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:58.200
regions that are extremely
far away from each other.

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:00.210
There is the
imposition of Mandarin,

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:03.240
one standard language at the
detriment of local languages.

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:05.850
Everything is to do
with unification,

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:08.460
with the imposition of one
particular standard, one

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:12.670
culture, one time zone,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:15.660
But I wish to say something
about Sun Yat Sen.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:17.830
He may very well have said that.

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:23.160
But the truth is that after
the empire collapsed in 1911,

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:28.810
China was the very
first republic in Asia.

00:44:28.810 --> 00:44:35.600
Some 40 million people voted
in 1912, 10% of the population.

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:37.660
It was an electoral
representation

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:43.360
that was not reached
by Japan till 1928,

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:46.640
I think, India till 1935.

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:50.680
There was a huge
interest from 1912

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:56.760
until 1949 in
participatory democracy.

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:58.890
There were civil associations.

00:44:58.890 --> 00:45:03.240
There was an attempt to
separate out powers and build up

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:05.460
an independent judicial system.

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:09.810
All too often we skip the entire
first half of the 20th century

00:45:09.810 --> 00:45:12.870
as if Mao somehow
came from nowhere.

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:16.520
But the truth is that
the Republic of China,

00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:21.630
the period from 1912
to 1949, demonstrates

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:27.150
that China can perfectly well
be a democratic, open, thriving

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:28.343
society.

00:45:28.343 --> 00:45:30.010
And that's what we
have to bear in mind.

00:45:34.930 --> 00:45:36.250
AUDIENCE: All right, sir.

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:37.667
Thank you for
coming to Hillsdale.

00:45:37.667 --> 00:45:38.890
I appreciate you coming.

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:43.458
So trying to be pragmatic
here in September of 2019,

00:45:43.458 --> 00:45:45.250
you mentioned right at
the end of your talk

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:48.820
about not engaging
in a China fantasy

00:45:48.820 --> 00:45:51.380
with the current administration.

00:45:51.380 --> 00:45:53.230
So I want to hear
your thoughts on what

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:56.230
we're doing right,
what we're doing wrong,

00:45:56.230 --> 00:45:59.260
how our current
dispute with China

00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:02.110
is likely to end, specifically
with the tariffs and the trade

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:02.610
war.

00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:06.070
So I wanted to see what
your thoughts were on that.

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:08.320
FRANK DIKOTTER: Well, I
don't know how it will end.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:09.130
I'm a historian.

00:46:09.130 --> 00:46:10.780
I study the past,
not the future.

00:46:13.480 --> 00:46:16.510
But you want my guess.

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:19.780
Well, as I pointed out at
the very end of my talk,

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:21.460
for the first time
you have a president

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:29.770
who seems to know
how to unsettle

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:33.795
that regime profoundly.

00:46:33.795 --> 00:46:35.743
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:42.200
And you have
somebody who appears

00:46:42.200 --> 00:46:46.820
to understand that the biggest
threat to your freedom,

00:46:46.820 --> 00:46:50.060
and mine as well, comes from
the People's Republic of China

00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:53.150
with the largest organization
on Planet Earth devoted

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:59.740
to crushing the very values
of freedom and democracy.

00:46:59.740 --> 00:47:04.260
Now is the trade war the
right way of doing this?

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:07.050
Well, my understanding of
Adam Smith and free trade

00:47:07.050 --> 00:47:10.060
is that we live on an island.

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:11.310
Now you're good at fishing.

00:47:11.310 --> 00:47:13.710
You have five fish.

00:47:13.710 --> 00:47:15.840
I have five coconuts every day.

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.690
We exchange.

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:19.830
You're wealthier, I'm wealthier.

00:47:19.830 --> 00:47:25.020
But as I pointed out,
when Europeans buy stuff

00:47:25.020 --> 00:47:28.980
from China, when Americans
buy stuff from China,

00:47:28.980 --> 00:47:31.980
the theory would be that
Chinese people will buy stuff

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:34.260
from Europe and stuff
from the United States.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:35.490
But they don't.

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:37.470
It's called
financial repression.

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:39.930
The wealth flows to the party.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:42.840
Their bank accounts
belong to the state.

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:47.370
The state does as it sees fit
with the money of other people.

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:50.140
It will build these
empty airports.

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:52.590
It will build these
empty railways.

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:56.290
It will build up that
mighty army it has.

00:47:56.290 --> 00:47:59.250
So in that sense,
it's not free trade,

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:03.010
and it most certainly
isn't fair trade.

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:05.010
So again, the trade
war seems to me

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:10.720
to be a reasonable approach,
all the more if you study

00:48:10.720 --> 00:48:14.130
the economy of China
since 1979 and you

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:16.650
realize how
extraordinarily dependent

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:20.610
they are on earning
foreign currency.

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:25.360
In the beginning, I said
everything goes back to Stalin.

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:27.000
We may think of
the economy today

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:29.430
as being utterly
different, but what

00:48:29.430 --> 00:48:33.090
did Stalin do with
the first famine,

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:38.010
first Communist
famine, '29, '32, '33?

00:48:38.010 --> 00:48:42.780
He took massive stocks of
food from the countryside

00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:47.030
to sell it on the
international market

00:48:47.030 --> 00:48:52.220
in order to buy the
industrial equipment needed

00:48:52.220 --> 00:48:55.740
to modernize the Soviet Union.

00:48:55.740 --> 00:48:57.630
He wanted foreign currency.

00:48:57.630 --> 00:48:59.250
That's exactly what Mao did.

00:48:59.250 --> 00:49:01.140
That's exactly what Deng did.

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:03.090
That's exactly what Xi does.

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:05.910
The only difference,
under Mao, trade

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:07.290
would be with the
Socialist bloc.

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:09.790
Under Deng, it's with
the rest of the world.

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:14.260
But this is a machine
devoted to making sure

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:17.290
that China can sell
as much as possible

00:49:17.290 --> 00:49:19.540
at a very small margin.

00:49:19.540 --> 00:49:20.950
There are tax rebates.

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:22.500
There are subsidies.

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:27.140
There are massive, huge
state structures behind it.

00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:29.110
And all of that
foreign currency is

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:33.400
needed to keep the rest
of the country afloat.

00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:38.430
So if you poke a hole
in that, then you

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.490
see the leaders
in Beijing scurry

00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:46.450
because they are so incredibly
dependent on you and your US

00:49:46.450 --> 00:49:49.970
dollars that you will
weaken them very quickly.

00:49:56.260 --> 00:49:58.200
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for coming out tonight.

00:49:58.200 --> 00:50:00.492
I have a question pertaining
to the issue of Hong Kong,

00:50:00.492 --> 00:50:02.490
and ultimately the
ideology of China.

00:50:02.490 --> 00:50:05.126
It seems one consistent
theme throughout.

00:50:05.126 --> 00:50:06.126
Want me to just hold it?

00:50:06.126 --> 00:50:06.840
SPEAKER 1: I'm supposed
to hold it, I'm sorry.

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:08.250
AUDIENCE: OK, gotcha.

00:50:08.250 --> 00:50:08.993
Yeah.

00:50:08.993 --> 00:50:10.410
It seems like one
consistent theme

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:12.160
is the method doesn't matter.

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:13.930
The only thing that
matters is the result,

00:50:13.930 --> 00:50:16.680
which is more power or
control over the people,

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:18.390
whether it be
modernisation or literally

00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:20.010
tearing down a culture.

00:50:20.010 --> 00:50:23.190
How, then, can the United States
defend cities like Hong Kong,

00:50:23.190 --> 00:50:26.310
or an entire
quasi-country like Taiwan,

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:29.970
when the government literally
does not care about the means,

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:32.190
but securing what they want?

00:50:32.190 --> 00:50:34.170
Is there any actual
strategy we can draw up,

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:36.750
or is it just simply
like playing checkers

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:38.640
back and forth?

00:50:38.640 --> 00:50:41.730
FRANK DIKOTTER: Well, as I said,
don't overestimate your enemy.

00:50:41.730 --> 00:50:44.590
Don't underestimate him.

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:46.390
We may think that
Hong Kong is just

00:50:46.390 --> 00:50:52.480
a small city of eight
million people with its laws,

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:55.030
with its independent
judicial system.

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:58.367
And across the border
is 1.4 billion people.

00:50:58.367 --> 00:50:59.950
They call it one
country, two systems.

00:50:59.950 --> 00:51:00.730
Really?

00:51:00.730 --> 00:51:02.590
Seems a little
unbalanced, doesn't it?

00:51:02.590 --> 00:51:05.620
Hardly fair for
eight million people

00:51:05.620 --> 00:51:09.220
to put up a fight to preserve--
not acquire new freedoms,

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:12.300
preserve the ones they have.

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:15.330
What can you do if
that 1.4 million

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:18.900
machine is determined
to crush Hong Kong?

00:51:18.900 --> 00:51:21.400
Well, guess what?

00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:23.420
Goes back to what
I said earlier on.

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:27.410
The wealth goes to the
party and party members.

00:51:27.410 --> 00:51:30.950
Do you think they have any
faith in their own system?

00:51:30.950 --> 00:51:31.580
No.

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:34.760
These billionaires, who
I mentioned occasionally

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:38.300
disappear, and their wealth,
from one day to the next,

00:51:38.300 --> 00:51:40.540
becomes state property.

00:51:40.540 --> 00:51:44.160
If you're smart, you have money,
you place it in Hong Kong.

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:47.720
There are thousands of companies
in Hong Kong from the mainland.

00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:51.500
Some 60% of inward investment
comes from Hong Kong.

00:51:51.500 --> 00:51:54.170
60% of outward investment
goes through Hong Kong.

00:51:54.170 --> 00:51:55.320
It's vital.

00:51:55.320 --> 00:51:58.730
They've tried to replicate it
with Shenzhen and Shanghai,

00:51:58.730 --> 00:52:00.800
and they've failed
because they don't

00:52:00.800 --> 00:52:03.930
have separation of powers.

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:08.690
So sometimes it just seems
like you're fighting a monster

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:11.000
that you can't beat.

00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:14.380
But remember, the Soviet
Union was the same story

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:16.910
in the 1970s.

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:18.620
People would come
back from Siberia

00:52:18.620 --> 00:52:21.250
and say, oh, these Soviets.

00:52:21.250 --> 00:52:24.300
They're going to
overtake the world.

00:52:24.300 --> 00:52:27.220
They've got a system
we can't beat.

00:52:27.220 --> 00:52:30.980
They crush democracy.

00:52:30.980 --> 00:52:34.378
But the result is a strong man.

00:52:34.378 --> 00:52:36.420
They can do things we
simply can't get away with.

00:52:39.250 --> 00:52:40.450
It's the lost fight.

00:52:40.450 --> 00:52:42.490
How could you
possibly fight them?

00:52:42.490 --> 00:52:45.490
Well, it disintegrated,
more or less,

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:51.210
with a bit of help from another
chap who's out there, Ronald.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:53.084
He's a bit fenced off, though.

00:52:56.500 --> 00:53:02.490
So there are many tools that you
can use to fight the one party

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:06.060
state, and many of these
tools actually come straight--

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:09.030
in Europe, in this country--

00:53:09.030 --> 00:53:14.000
from our experience
of the Cold War.

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:18.050
And one approach
of the Cold War is

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:20.750
to have one standard
for everyone, not

00:53:20.750 --> 00:53:22.370
a double standard.

00:53:22.370 --> 00:53:24.430
And this is the issue.

00:53:24.430 --> 00:53:27.160
So much knowledge about the
Soviet Union, and the moment

00:53:27.160 --> 00:53:29.880
we talk about China, it's as
if all that knowledge just

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:31.140
evaporates.

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:32.130
Why?

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:34.380
Because when we used to
talk about the Soviet Union,

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:37.395
it was about politics,
not about culture.

00:53:37.395 --> 00:53:39.020
Nobody in his right
mind would stand up

00:53:39.020 --> 00:53:42.890
and say the Soviet Union is a
different culture, you know?

00:53:42.890 --> 00:53:45.070
It's the Orthodox Church.

00:53:45.070 --> 00:53:48.200
No, the Orthodox
Church was crushed.

00:53:48.200 --> 00:53:49.830
Survived.

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:51.520
Now we talk about China.

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:52.890
It's the exact opposite.

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:53.970
It's not about politics.

00:53:53.970 --> 00:53:55.890
It's about culture.

00:53:55.890 --> 00:53:57.560
Read Henry Kissinger.

00:53:57.560 --> 00:53:58.810
They're not really communists.

00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.005
It's Confucian culture.

00:54:01.005 --> 00:54:02.130
These people are wonderful.

00:54:02.130 --> 00:54:04.360
They're not true communists.

00:54:04.360 --> 00:54:07.650
Three presidents said this.

00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:14.810
Roosevelt. He thought all these
are just agrarian reformers,

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:18.384
so let's not help
Chiang Kai-shek.

00:54:18.384 --> 00:54:21.680
Mao Tze Tung is a good chap.

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:25.580
Well, a few years later they
were fighting them in Korea.

00:54:25.580 --> 00:54:28.400
Didn't go all that well.

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:30.740
Nixon, Kissinger, same story.

00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:33.840
Oh, this Confucian
culture, they're

00:54:33.840 --> 00:54:34.950
not like the Soviet Union.

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:40.860
We should use them
against the Soviet Union,

00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:43.590
allow them access
under Clinton--

00:54:43.590 --> 00:54:45.090
under Reagan, I'm sorry to say--

00:54:45.090 --> 00:54:50.150
allow them access to our
technology, sell them weapons,

00:54:50.150 --> 00:54:52.280
have the students do
things we would never

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:57.340
do with the Soviet Union till
it collapsed in '89, 1991.

00:54:57.340 --> 00:55:03.210
And Clinton '94 after the
Tiananmen massacre, same story.

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:05.600
Help them with their
economy, and they

00:55:05.600 --> 00:55:10.640
will reform as surely as
the cart follows the ox.

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:12.050
Didn't happen, either.

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:14.690
One standard.

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:17.650
You sign a treaty, you
respect the treaty.

00:55:17.650 --> 00:55:21.140
You say you will do something,
you stick to your word.

00:55:21.140 --> 00:55:23.150
One standard for
treatment of human beings.

00:55:26.066 --> 00:55:32.880
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:35.010
SPEAKER 1: Are you OK
for a few more questions?

00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:36.650
FRANK DIKOTTER: I'm happy to
go on for as long as you want.

00:55:36.650 --> 00:55:38.970
As far as I'm concerned,
it's 2:00 in the morning, so.

00:55:42.978 --> 00:55:44.770
AUDIENCE: I hope it
doesn't take that long.

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:48.390
I'm not very good at this.

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:54.330
About 25 some years ago,
I established a 100%

00:55:54.330 --> 00:55:57.220
foreign owned company in China.

00:55:57.220 --> 00:56:02.130
And about a year ago, I sold it.

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:14.310
And I engaged my wife's
help, who is Chinese.

00:56:14.310 --> 00:56:18.300
I engaged a local, very
high level accountant

00:56:18.300 --> 00:56:28.770
to advise me on how to deal
with the cash, several millions

00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:30.150
of dollars.

00:56:30.150 --> 00:56:35.850
And so well, maybe, you know,
buy a new car or something?

00:56:35.850 --> 00:56:40.680
He said, oh, no, you
better not do that.

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:43.060
We have to be very
careful with this.

00:56:43.060 --> 00:56:53.190
If this is not spent just
exactly, you can go to jail.

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:55.320
Go to jail?

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:56.010
The question.

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:57.010
I'm going to get
to the question.

00:56:57.010 --> 00:56:57.510
I'm sorry.

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:04.350
So I said, well,
my children have

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:08.070
$100,000 tuition coming up.

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:09.600
Can we spend on that?

00:57:09.600 --> 00:57:10.880
Oh, sure, no problem.

00:57:18.430 --> 00:57:25.000
Is China ever going to allow
me to spend that money how

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:29.360
I would like to without
having to go to jail?

00:57:29.360 --> 00:57:30.120
I mean, it's--

00:57:30.120 --> 00:57:31.870
FRANK DIKOTTER: I will
give you my advice.

00:57:31.870 --> 00:57:33.510
AUDIENCE: It's very daunting.

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:38.030
FRANK DIKOTTER:
For a $1,000 fee.

00:57:38.030 --> 00:57:40.300
I don't think you'll go to jail.

00:57:40.300 --> 00:57:43.840
But all these
billionaires in China

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:46.630
are a lot smarter than you
are because they grew up

00:57:46.630 --> 00:57:51.850
in this system, and they
are desperate to get

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:54.700
their money out of the country.

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:58.120
So chances of you succeeding are
pretty slim, I'm sorry to say.

00:58:00.897 --> 00:58:01.730
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:04.210
We have time for
one more question.

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:07.240
FRANK DIKOTTER: Now capital,
capitalism, free movement

00:58:07.240 --> 00:58:10.542
of goods, people, not in China.

00:58:10.542 --> 00:58:13.000
JAMES MILIUS: I want to thank
you again for coming out here

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:13.660
tonight.

00:58:13.660 --> 00:58:18.820
So you've spoken a lot about the
ways in which Mao took things

00:58:18.820 --> 00:58:21.460
from Stalin's ideology and
Stalin's implementation

00:58:21.460 --> 00:58:22.780
of policies.

00:58:22.780 --> 00:58:24.490
However, I know
that those who are

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:27.680
sympathetic to Mao or
to the Chinese system

00:58:27.680 --> 00:58:30.040
would be quick to point out
differences between what

00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:33.310
Mao and Stalin did, and would
point out that they often

00:58:33.310 --> 00:58:34.863
disagreed on certain things.

00:58:34.863 --> 00:58:36.280
I was wondering
if maybe you could

00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:38.492
speak a little bit
towards to what extent

00:58:38.492 --> 00:58:40.450
some of those differences
might be substantial,

00:58:40.450 --> 00:58:42.490
and to what extent
they are superficial.

00:58:42.490 --> 00:58:45.460
FRANK DIKOTTER: You
want another lecture?

00:58:45.460 --> 00:58:46.750
I'm happy to give you one.

00:58:51.370 --> 00:58:57.400
I talked briefly about how Mao
destroyed every organization

00:58:57.400 --> 00:59:01.280
outside of the organization of
the Communist Party of China.

00:59:01.280 --> 00:59:05.640
What do you think Adolf
Hitler did after 1933?

00:59:05.640 --> 00:59:09.890
Here's your German word
of the day, [GERMAN],,

00:59:09.890 --> 00:59:12.070
a process of
synchronization in which

00:59:12.070 --> 00:59:14.860
the Nazis, over the course
of two, three years,

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:17.230
take over every
single institution

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:21.670
down to your local sports club.

00:59:21.670 --> 00:59:24.010
Now all culture is
eliminated and replaced

00:59:24.010 --> 00:59:27.460
with Nazi paraphernalia.

00:59:27.460 --> 00:59:29.470
Where did he get it from?

00:59:29.470 --> 00:59:30.880
He got it from Lenin.

00:59:30.880 --> 00:59:33.440
Lenin was the
first one to do it.

00:59:33.440 --> 00:59:36.620
Ultimately, of course,
these are different.

00:59:36.620 --> 00:59:41.320
The Mao of 1949 is
not the Mao of 1956.

00:59:41.320 --> 00:59:45.010
The differences between Mao
and Stalin are plentiful.

00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:47.080
You can find all
sorts of differences.

00:59:47.080 --> 00:59:51.760
But to emphasize difference is
to not see the overall picture.

00:59:51.760 --> 00:59:56.770
The overall picture, I think,
is reasonably straightforward.

00:59:56.770 --> 01:00:02.390
In the 20th century, you have
two trends that are opposed.

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:07.540
One is to separate out powers.

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:12.350
Goes back to that French
chap, Montesquieu.

01:00:12.350 --> 01:00:13.630
You do philosophy?

01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:14.720
That's what I thought.

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:15.530
And sociology.

01:00:18.120 --> 01:00:20.460
Tough.

01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:22.500
Montesquieu first
came up with the idea

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:24.030
of separation of powers.

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:25.770
Other philosophers did so, too.

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:29.340
For centuries in parts
of Europe, United

01:00:29.340 --> 01:00:32.320
States, other
parts of the world,

01:00:32.320 --> 01:00:35.100
people have been working
very hard at coming up

01:00:35.100 --> 01:00:39.540
with checks and balances, an
independent judicial system,

01:00:39.540 --> 01:00:42.320
complicated arrangements.

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:44.340
But sometimes you
have to hire a lawyer.

01:00:44.340 --> 01:00:46.920
Can Boris Johnson
do this or that?

01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:48.210
You need lawyers.

01:00:48.210 --> 01:00:49.910
Difficult. Not sure.

01:00:49.910 --> 01:00:51.790
You might end up in jail.

01:00:51.790 --> 01:00:55.260
It's a great achievement,
an extraordinary achievement

01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:59.200
for any government-- seen
from the Stone Age onwards,

01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:01.950
where human beings
club each other--

01:01:01.950 --> 01:01:04.710
for all of a sudden
the government

01:01:04.710 --> 01:01:09.090
to respect a court order is
an extraordinary achievement.

01:01:09.090 --> 01:01:10.120
That's one trend.

01:01:10.120 --> 01:01:12.360
The other trend is the
concentration of power.

01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:15.750
With Lenin, with Hitler,
Mussolini, you name it,

01:01:15.750 --> 01:01:19.950
these dictators think that all
that wishy-washy separation

01:01:19.950 --> 01:01:22.020
of powers only weakens
the entire entity.

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:24.330
What you ought to do
is concentrate powers

01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:28.450
in the hands of one party
represented by one person.

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:31.020
So yes, I could go
on about differences

01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:33.120
between Stalin and Mao.

01:01:33.120 --> 01:01:36.760
But ultimately, it's
the same mechanism.

01:01:36.760 --> 01:01:38.800
It's the same vision.

01:01:38.800 --> 01:01:44.470
It's the same desire to control
power and keep it firmly

01:01:44.470 --> 01:01:48.380
in the hands of the party state.

01:01:48.380 --> 01:01:51.620
I'd say one difference
is that Stalin,

01:01:51.620 --> 01:01:54.470
at the height of the
terror in the 1930s,

01:01:54.470 --> 01:01:57.250
at least had the courage to
sign all the death warrants.

01:01:57.250 --> 01:01:59.690
There were about
1.5 million of them.

01:01:59.690 --> 01:02:02.000
He had to sign these long
books with lists of names.

01:02:02.000 --> 01:02:04.070
He signed it.

01:02:04.070 --> 01:02:07.140
Mao pitted people
against each other.

01:02:07.140 --> 01:02:09.530
He made sure everybody
had blood on their hands.

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:10.910
But it's a minor detail.

01:02:10.910 --> 01:02:14.420
Ultimately, it's
the one party state

01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:16.520
that is barely different
from the beginning

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:19.121
of the century to the very end.

01:02:19.121 --> 01:02:20.657
SPEAKER 1: Thank
you, Dr. Dikotter.

01:02:20.657 --> 01:02:21.740
FRANK DIKOTTER: Thank you.

01:02:21.740 --> 01:02:24.190
[APPLAUSE]

